wet-001
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.900 region:lecture align:start
Good evening, and thank you all for coming out to

wet-002
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.700
the field station on a damp night like this one.

wet-003
00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.900
Tonight I want to walk you through the ecology of

wet-004
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.700
a temperate tidal freshwater marsh,

wet-005
00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.300
and rather than speak in the abstract I will keep

wet-006
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.700 line:0 position:10%
returning to one site as our running example,

wet-007
00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.700
the Marsh at Cormorant Bend,

wet-008
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.100
which sits where the Alder River finally slows and spreads

wet-009
00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.900
out just before it reaches the sea.

wet-010
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:29.100
Almost everything I describe tonight is drawn from field seasons

wet-011
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.900 align:middle
at that single marsh.

wet-012
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:40.500
The most important variable in any wetland,

wet-013
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.900
the one you must understand before anything else makes sense,

wet-014
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.900
is water. More precisely it is the hydroperiod,

wet-015
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.300
which is the pattern of how deep the water sits

wet-016
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.100 region:lecture
and for how long it stays.

wet-017
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.300
At Cormorant Bend the marsh floods and drains twice a

wet-018
00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.100
day on the tide,

wet-019
00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:00.700
and yet the water there stays essentially fresh,

wet-020
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.100
because the steady discharge of the Alder River holds the

wet-021
00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:06.100 region:lecture align:start
salt wedge from the sea penned downstream.

wet-022
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.500
We call a marsh like that oligohaline,

wet-023
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.300
meaning only very lightly salty,

wet-024
00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.500
usually &lt;&nbsp;0.5&nbsp;ppt, and that low salinity shapes every living thing

wet-025
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.300
in it.

wet-026
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:25.900 line:0 position:10%
Let me introduce you to the plants,

wet-027
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:28.300
because the plants are the architecture of the place.

wet-028
00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:31.300
The species that defines the low marsh at Cormorant Bend

wet-029
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.700
is <lang la>Lyngbye&#39;s&nbsp;sedge</lang>, a tall grasslike plant with sharp triangular

wet-030
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:36.500
stems that forms stands so dense you can barely push

wet-031
00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.700 align:middle
through them by the middle of August.

wet-032
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:42.500
Just a little higher up,

wet-033
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:46.100
on ground that floods less often,

wet-034
00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:48.500
the <i>broadleaf&nbsp;cattail</i> takes over,

wet-035
00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.500
with its familiar brown cigar shaped seed spikes standing above

wet-036
00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.900 region:lecture
the water. The boundary between the sedge and the cattail

wet-037
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:56.700
is not random. It tracks the elevation of the ground

wet-038
00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.900
almost to the centimeter,

wet-039
00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:02.700
because each species can only tolerate so many hours a

wet-040
00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:06.300
day with its roots underwater.

wet-041
00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:13.700 region:lecture align:start
Now here is the part that genuinely surprises people the

wet-042
00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:16.700
first time they hear it.

wet-043
00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:19.100
These marsh plants grow furiously through the summer,

wet-044
00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:21.900
die back every winter,

wet-045
00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:25.100
and yet their dead stems and roots do not fully

wet-046
00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:27.900 line:0 position:10%
rot away, because the waterlogged soil holds almost no oxygen

wet-047
00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:31.500
for the decomposers to breathe.

wet-048
00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.900
Year after year that half decayed plant material simply piles

wet-049
00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.900
up in place as a spongy dark soil we call

wet-050
00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.300
peat. At Cormorant Bend the peat is now nearly 2&nbsp;m

wet-051
00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:42.100 align:middle
deep in the oldest part of the marsh,

wet-052
00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:45.300
and it is still building upward at roughly 3&nbsp;mm every

wet-053
00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.100
year.

wet-054
00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:56.700
That slow burial has a consequence far larger than the

wet-055
00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.100
marsh itself. Because the carbon in those dead plants gets

wet-056
00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:02.100 region:lecture
locked away in the peat instead of returning to the

wet-057
00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.500
atmosphere as carbon dioxide,

wet-058
00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:07.300
the marsh acts as a genuine <b>carbon&nbsp;sink</b>.

wet-059
00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:10.500
Our best estimate is that Cormorant Bend pulls down and

wet-060
00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.300
stores on the order of 1.2&nbsp;tonnes of carbon per hectare

wet-061
00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:16.900 region:lecture align:start
every single year. Multiply that across every hectare of healthy

wet-062
00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:19.300
coastal marsh in the region and you begin to see

wet-063
00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:22.300
why people have started calling these places <b>blue&nbsp;carbon</b> ecosystems and

wet-064
00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:24.700
fighting so hard to protect them.

wet-065
00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.500
The lack of oxygen in the soil is not a

wet-066
00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:35.700 line:0 position:10%
defect, by the way.

wet-067
00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:38.500
It is the engine that drives some of the marsh&#39;s

wet-068
00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:42.100
most valuable chemistry. Just a centimeter or two below the

wet-069
00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:44.500
surface the soil turns anoxic,

wet-070
00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:47.500
and what a chemist would call the redox potential drops

wet-071
00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:49.900 align:middle
steeply into negative territory.

wet-072
00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.700
In exactly that zone,

wet-073
00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:55.900
specialized bacteria strip dissolved nitrogen straight out of the water

wet-074
00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:58.700
through a process called denitrification,

wet-075
00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:02.300
converting nitrate into ordinary,

wet-076
00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:04.700 region:lecture
harmless nitrogen gas that simply bubbles away into the air.

wet-077
00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:07.700
A marsh of this size can quietly remove a startling

wet-078
00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:10.100
load of the fertilizer runoff that would otherwise pour into

wet-079
00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:12.900
the bay and feed suffocating algae blooms.

wet-080
00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:21.100
There is a genuine tradeoff here,

wet-081
00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:23.900 region:lecture align:start
though, and I want to be honest about it rather

wet-082
00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:27.500
than sell you a fairy tale.

wet-083
00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.900
Those same waterlogged, oxygen starved soils that store all that

wet-084
00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:32.900
carbon are also the perfect home for microbes that produce

wet-085
00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:35.300
methane, which is a far more potent greenhouse gas than

wet-086
00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:38.100 line:0 position:10%
carbon dioxide over the short term.

wet-087
00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:41.300
So a freshwater marsh is simultaneously a <b>carbon&nbsp;sink</b> through its

wet-088
00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:44.100
peat and a methane source through its bacteria.

wet-089
00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:47.700
Which effect ultimately wins out depends heavily on salinity.

wet-090
00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:50.100
The saltier a marsh becomes,

wet-091
00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:53.100 align:middle
the more the sulfate in seawater suppresses those methane producing

wet-092
00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:55.500
microbes, which is one reason salt marshes often come out

wet-093
00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:58.300
looking better on the greenhouse ledger than freshwater ones.

wet-094
00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:06.500
Let me pull you back up out of the mud

wet-095
00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:09.300
and into the living,

wet-096
00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:12.900 region:lecture
breathing marsh, because it is spectacular at dawn.

wet-097
00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:15.300
The signature sound at Cormorant Bend is the call of

wet-098
00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:18.300
the banded marsh chorus frog,

wet-099
00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.700
a little amphibian barely the size of your thumbnail,

wet-100
00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:23.500
whose call sounds exactly like a fingernail dragged slowly down

wet-101
00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:26.700 region:lecture align:start
the teeth of a comb.

wet-102
00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:29.500
On a still April morning a thousand of them calling

wet-103
00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:33.100
together is genuinely deafening.

wet-104
00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:35.500
They breed in the shallow warm pools that linger for

wet-105
00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:38.500
a few weeks after the big spring tides,

wet-106
00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:40.900 line:0 position:10%
and their tadpoles have to race to grow legs before

wet-107
00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:43.700
those pools dry down.

wet-108
00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:51.900
Beneath the surface, the mud itself is astonishingly alive.

wet-109
00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:54.700
A single handful holds a whole community of macroinvertebrates,

wet-110
00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:58.300
the amphipods and midge larvae and tiny snails that most

wet-111
00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:00.700 align:middle
visitors never notice. Those small animals are the hidden engine

wet-112
00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:03.700
of the food web,

wet-113
00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:06.100
because they shred and consume the enormous quantity of dead

wet-114
00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:08.900
plant material, the detritus,

wet-115
00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:12.100
that the sedge and cattail drop every autumn.

wet-116
00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:14.900 region:lecture
In a marsh like this one the food web is

wet-117
00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:18.500
built from the bottom up on decaying plants rather than

wet-118
00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:20.900
on living green leaves,

NOTE SYNTHETIC - speaker pauses for a slide change.

wet-119
00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:23.900
and that detritus based foundation is what makes these wetlands

wet-120
00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:26.300
so extraordinarily productive.

wet-121
00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:34.100 region:lecture align:start
All of that invertebrate life is,

wet-122
00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:37.300
in turn, food for birds,

wet-123
00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:40.100
and the marsh feeds an enormous number of them.

wet-124
00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:43.700
The most secretive resident is the American bittern,

wet-125
00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:46.100
a stocky heron that,

wet-126
00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:49.100 line:0 position:10%
when it feels threatened,

wet-127
00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:51.500
freezes bolt upright with its bill pointed straight at the

wet-128
00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:54.300
sky and simply vanishes against the vertical reeds.

wet-129
00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:57.500
In the fall, tens of thousands of migrating ducks and

wet-130
00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:00.300
sandpipers stage at Cormorant Bend,

wet-131
00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:03.900 align:middle
refueling on the fallen seeds and the fat rich invertebrates

wet-132
00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:06.300
before they push on south for the winter.

wet-133
00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:09.300
Lose the marsh and you do not just lose the

wet-134
00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.700
frogs, you punch a hole in a flyway that stretches

wet-135
00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:14.500
across a continent.

wet-136
00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:22.700 region:lecture
The tidal channels that thread through the marsh are nurseries

wet-137
00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:25.500
in their own right.

wet-138
00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:29.100
On a single net haul last June we counted more

wet-139
00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:31.500
than four hundred juvenile salmon sheltering in one channel at

wet-140
00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:34.500
Cormorant Bend, alongside sticklebacks and the little killifish we call

wet-141
00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:36.900 region:lecture align:start
mummichogs. Those young fish fatten on marsh insects for weeks,

wet-142
00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:39.700
hiding from bigger predators in the shallow water,

wet-143
00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:42.900
before they are large enough to survive out in the

wet-144
00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:45.700
open bay.

wet-145
00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:54.300
We also keep a close eye on the water itself.

wet-146
00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:56.700 line:0 position:10%
Once a month a student walks a fixed transect across

wet-147
00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:59.700
the marsh with a handheld sonde,

wet-148
00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:02.100
logging the temperature, the salinity,

wet-149
00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:04.900
and the dissolved oxygen at each numbered stake.

wet-150
00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:08.100
It was that patient monthly record that first caught the

wet-151
00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:10.900 align:middle
oxygen crash of the summer of 2020,

wet-152
00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:14.500
when a warm, still week drove the dissolved oxygen down

wet-153
00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:16.900
low enough to leave the fish gasping at the surface.

wet-154
00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:24.900
And the marsh quietly protects the town behind it.

wet-155
00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:27.300
When the big storm surge of 2021 pushed up the

wet-156
00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:30.100 region:lecture
Alder estuary, the broad,

wet-157
00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:33.300
shallow marsh soaked up the water and dragged its speed

wet-158
00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:36.100
down, and the flood that finally reached the village was

wet-159
00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:39.700
measurably lower than the engineers had feared.

wet-160
00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:42.100
A healthy marsh is a seawall that rebuilds itself for

wet-161
00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:45.100 region:lecture align:start
free, every single tide.

wet-162
00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:52.500
No honest account of this place is complete without a

wet-163
00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:55.300
warning, so here it is.

wet-164
00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:58.500
An aggressive introduced strain of the common reed,

wet-165
00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:01.300
<i>Phragmites&nbsp;australis</i>, has begun marching along the drier upland edge of

wet-166
00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:04.900 line:0 position:10%
the marsh. It grows in towering,

wet-167
00:09:04.900 --> 00:09:07.300
ferociously dense monocultures that shade out and crowd the native

wet-168
00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:10.300
sedge and cattail, and it offers far less food and

wet-169
00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:12.700
shelter to wildlife in return.

wet-170
00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:15.500
Once a stand of it is truly established,

wet-171
00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:18.700 align:middle
it is extraordinarily difficult and expensive to remove,

wet-172
00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:21.500
so the cheapest victory is always to catch it early.

wet-173
00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:30.100
That threat brings me to the restoration story I most

wet-174
00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:32.500
love to tell. Back in 2019 the old Alder Slough

wet-175
00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:35.500
levee, an earthen dike that had cut the northern third

wet-176
00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:37.900 region:lecture
of the marsh off from the tide for almost seventy

wet-177
00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:40.700
years, was deliberately breached by the conservancy in three carefully

wet-178
00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:43.900
placed cuts. The results came faster than anyone dared hope.

wet-179
00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:46.700
Within two growing seasons the sedge had recolonized the reconnected

wet-180
00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:50.300
flats on its own,

wet-181
00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:52.700 region:lecture align:start
juvenile salmon and stickleback were using the new tidal channels,

wet-182
00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:55.700
and the bare subsided ground had begun trapping sediment and

wet-183
00:09:55.700 --> 00:09:58.100
building back upward again.

wet-184
00:09:58.100 --> 00:10:00.900
It is one of the clearest restoration success stories anywhere

wet-185
00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:04.100
in the region.

wet-186
00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:11.900 line:0 position:10%
I should tell you how we actually know the marsh

wet-187
00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:15.500
is building upward, since I keep quoting that 3&nbsp;mm a

wet-188
00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:17.900
year. We measure it directly.

wet-189
00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:20.900
We lay down a thin bright marker horizon of white

wet-190
00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:23.300
feldspar clay on the marsh surface,

wet-191
00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:26.100 align:middle
then come back years later and cut a tiny core

wet-192
00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:29.300
straight through it to see exactly how much fresh sediment

wet-193
00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:32.100
has buried the layer.

wet-194
00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:35.700
We pair that with elevation surveys using survey grade satellite

wet-195
00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:38.100
positioning accurate to a few millimeters.

wet-196
00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:41.100 region:lecture
It is slow, patient,

wet-197
00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:43.500
muddy work, and it is the only way to answer

wet-198
00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:46.300
the question that matters most.

wet-199
00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:54.500
And that question, the one that honestly keeps me awake

wet-200
00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:57.300
at night, is sea level rise.

wet-201
00:10:57.300 --> 00:11:00.900 region:lecture align:start
Cormorant Bend can build itself upward at about 3&nbsp;mm a

wet-202
00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:03.300
year, as I said,

wet-203
00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:06.300
but if the sea begins to rise faster than the

wet-204
00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:08.700
marsh can accrete, the marsh will eventually drown in place,

wet-205
00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:11.500
its plants stranded underwater too long to survive.

wet-206
00:11:11.500 --> 00:11:14.700 line:0 position:10%
Its only escape is to migrate slowly inland onto higher

wet-207
00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:17.500
ground, and it can only do that if we have

wet-208
00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:21.100
left it somewhere to go.

wet-209
00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:23.500
Protecting that upland buffer,

wet-210
00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:26.500
the belt of open low lying ground just above today&#39;s

wet-211
00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:28.900 align:middle
marsh, may honestly be the single most important thing we

wet-212
00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:31.700
can do for Cormorant Bend over the next fifty years.

wet-213
00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:34.900
Thank you for listening,

wet-214
00:11:34.900 --> 00:11:37.700
and I would be glad to take your questions.
